## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of streaming quantiles and the trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and latency. It also evaluates familiarity with mergeable sketches for distributed systems.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming quantiles (TDigest/KLL)
- Mergeable data sketches across shards
- Compression parameter and accuracy vs memory trade-offs
- Update and query time complexities

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: TDigest usage
class TDigest { 
  add(x); 
  quantile(p); 
  merge(other); 
}

const digest = new TDigest(100);
for (const latency of batch) {
  digest.add(latency);
}
const p95 = digest.quantile(0.95);
```

## Performance Comparison
- **TDigest**: O(n log k) total time, O(k) memory, O(log k) per-element update
- **Recomputing**: O(n log n) total time, O(n) memory, O(1) per-element (but requires storage)

The streaming approach enables real-time 95th percentile computation with strict memory limits and supports distributed merging across shards.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming cardinality with bounded memory using probabilistic data structures; beginner-friendly but shows real trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for distinct count estimation
- Hashing, leading-zero counting, registers
- Time: O(1) per item, Memory: O(m)
- Error ~1.04/√m; choose m from m ≈ (1.04/ε)^2

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal HyperLogLog sketch (illustrative)
class HLL {
  constructor(p = 12) {
    this.m = 1 << p;
    this.reg = new Uint8Array(this.m);
  }
  add(item) {
    const h = hash64(item); // 64-bit int hash
    const p = Math.log2(this.m);
    const j = Number(h >>> (64 - p));
    const w = h & ((1n << BigInt(64 - p)) - 1n);
    const rank = 1 + clz64(w);
    if (this.reg[j] < rank) this.reg[j] = rank;
  }
  estimate() {
    // bias-corrected estimator placeholder
  }
}
```

