## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to balance space, accuracy, and latency for streaming distinct counts under a sliding window constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Counting Bloom Filter with aging counters
- Sliding window semantics and estimation bias
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs: probabilistic versus exact data structures

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCBF {
  constructor(m, k, windowSize) {
    this.counters = new Array(m).fill(0);
    this.ringBuffer = new Array(windowSize);
    this.head = 0;
    this.k = k;
  }

  hash(id, j) {
    const h = (id * 31 + j * 17) % m;
    return Math.abs(h);
  }

  add(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]++;
    }
    this.ringBuffer[this.head] = id;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.ringBuffer.length;
  }

  remove(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]--;
    }
  }

  estimateDistinct() {
    return this.counters.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / this.k;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of approximate quantiles with streaming data, a common constraint in production systems. It compares simple histograms to exact tracking and asks for concrete complexity and error bounds.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms for quantiles
- Per-element O(1) updates, O(W) queries
- Error bounds from bucket width w

## Code Example
```javascript
function HistQuantile(m, W) {
  const bucket = new Array(W).fill(0);
  this.n = 0; this.m = m; this.W = W;
  const w = Math.ceil(m / W);
  this.update = (x) => { const idx = Math.min(Math.floor(x / w), W-1); bucket[idx]++; this.n++; };
  this.median = () => {
    let target = Math.floor(this.n / 2) + 1;
    let cum = 0; let i = 0; const bw = Math.ceil(m / W);
    for (; i < W; i++) { if (cum + bucket[i] >= target) break; cum += bucket[i]; }
    const L = i * bw; const left = target - cum;
    const est = left <= bucket[i] ? L + (left / bucket[i]) * bw : L + bw;
    return est;
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust for skewed data?  
- How does choosing W affect error vs memory?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of online quantile estimation using the P² algorithm, a space-efficient alternative to GK-style sketches. It tests practical knowledge of update mechanics, error behavior, and the trade-offs between approximation and exact computation in streaming contexts.

## Key Concepts
- The P² algorithm uses five fixed-position marker heights to approximate quantiles
- Piecewise-linear interpolation between markers yields quantile estimates
- Per-element updates are constant time with fixed memory overhead
- The algorithm provides guarantees that improve with larger sample sizes

## Code Example
```javascript
class P2Quantile {
  constructor(quantile = 0.5) {
    this.quantile = quantile;
    this.n = 0;
    this.markers = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0];
    this.positions = [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1].map(p => p * 2);
  }

  update(value) {
    this.n++;
    if (this.n < 5) {
      this.markers[this.n - 1] = value;
      this.markers.sort((a, b) => a - b);
      return;
    }

    let j = this.quantile < 0.5 ? 1 : 3;
    if (value >= this.markers[j]) j++;

    for (let i = j; i < 5; i++) this.positions[i]++;

    for (let i = 1; i < 4; i++) {
      const d = this.positions[i] - this.positions[i-1];
      const target = (i + 1) * this.n / 4;
      if ((d > 1 && this.positions[i] < target) || 
          (d < 1 && this.positions[i] > target)) {
        const sign = d > 1 ? 1 : -1;
        this.markers[i] += sign * Math.min(d, Math.abs(this.positions[i] - target));
        this.positions[i] += sign;
      }
    }
  }

  getQuantile() {
    return this.markers[2];
  }
}
```

## Error Analysis
The P² algorithm exhibits decreasing error as more elements are processed. For uniform distributions, error is minimal; for skewed distributions, error may be slightly higher but remains bounded. Larger batch sizes reduce the frequency of updates but do not fundamentally alter the error characteristics.

## Comparison to Sorted Buffer
Maintaining a full sorted buffer of size N provides exact median computation but requires O(N) memory and O(log N) insertion time per element. The P² algorithm trades this exactness for constant memory and constant update time, making it suitable for memory-constrained streaming applications where approximate quantiles suffice.

## Why This Is Asked
Maintaining the number of distinct substrings under incremental appends tests knowledge of suffix automata, amortized analysis, and practical update strategies. It highlights how to avoid recomputation and reason about state growth.

## Key Concepts
- Suffix Automaton (SAM) for distinct substrings
- Online extensions and delta = len(cur) - len(link(cur))
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Space linear in string length

## Code Example
```javascript
class SAMNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = {};
    this.link = -1;
    this.len = 0;
  }
}
class SuffixAutomaton {
  constructor() {
    this.st = [new SAMNode()];
    this.last = 0;
    this.size = 1;
  }
  extend(c){
    const st = this.st; const cur = this.size++;
    st.push(new SAMNode()); st[cur].len = st[this.last].len + 1;
    let p = this.last;
    while (p >= 0 && !(c in st[p].next)) { st[p].next[c] = cur; p = st[p].link; }
    if (p === -1) { st[cur].link = 0; }
    else {
      const q = st[p].next[c];
      if (st[p].len + 1 === st[q].len) {
        st[cur].link = q;
      } else {
        const clone = this.size++;
        st.push(Object.assign(new SAMNode(), st[q]));
        st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
        while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] === q) { st[p].next[c] = clone; p = st[p].link; }
        st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
      }
    }
    this.last = cur;
  }
}
```

